/*! \page developer_backend_field_page Field and FFT Classes

\ref developer_backend_complex_page (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_math_page    (Next)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

The most important data structures used by the PSCF programs for 
periodic systems are arrays that store values of periodic fields 
(i.e., functions of position) on the nodes of a regular periodic 
mesh.  PSCF defines a set of class templates in namespace Pscf::Prdc 
that represent such periodic fields and Fourier transforms of such 
fields.  This namespace also contains a definition of a template for 
a class named Prdc::FFT that provides member functions that perform 
forward and inverse fast Fourier transform on such fields.  

Each of these class templates takes the dimension of space, denoted by 
D, and a backend identifier class, denoted by T, as their only template
parameters.  For example, the class template RField represents a 
real-valued field defined on the nodes of periodic mesh, and is declared
within the Pscf::Prdc namespace as a template 
\code
     template <int D, class T> class RField;
\endcode
The specialization RField \<3, CUT\> is thus a container for a real field 
defined on a 3-dimensional periodic mesh that allocates an underlying 
array in GPU global memory. A specialization RField \<2, CPT\> instead a 
container for a 2D real field that allocates an array in CPU heap memory.

The Pscf::Prdc namespace defines four basic class templates named 
RField, RFieldDft, CField, and FFT to store and manipulate periodic 
fields. Each of these four class templates takes the integer dimension 
of space D and a backend identifier class T as template parameters:

   - An RField \<D, T\> object contains values of a real-valued 
     periodic field on the nodes of a regular periodic D-dimensional 
     mesh.

   - An RFieldDft \<D, T\> object contains complex values of the 
     discrete Fourier transform (DFT) of a real-valued periodic field.

   - A CField \<D, T\> object can store either the values of a 
     complex-valued field on the nodes of a D-dimensional periodic 
     mesh, or the DFT of such a field. 

   - FFT \<D,T\> is a wrapper around a fast Fourier transform library
     that can transform a RField \<D,T\> to or from an RFieldDft \<D,T\>, 
     or that can apply a forward or inverse complex-to-complex Fourier 
     transform to a CField \<D, T\> object.

For each of these templates, D must be an integer 1, 2, 3, and T must 
be either Pscf::CPT or Pscf::CUT.  

The RFieldDft and CField class templates both be used to store arrays 
of complex numbers that represent discrete Fourier transform (DFT) of 
a field defined on a real-space mesh. A specialized class template 
RFieldDft is defined to store the DFT of a real field because the 
number of complex values needed to represent the DFT of a real field 
is approximately half the number of nodes in the real-space mesh on 
which the real-space field was defined, requiring the use of a container 
for which the dimension of the Fourier-space mesh used to defined the
DFT is different from the dimensions of the corresponding real-space 
mesh. The class template CField can, however, be used to store either 
a complex field or its DFT, because the DFT of a complex-valued field 
is stored on a Fourier-space mesh with the same dimensions as the 
corresponding real-space mesh.

\section developer_backend_field_field_sec Field Class Templates

Specializations of the three field class templates RField, RFieldDft, and 
CField are derived from corresponding specializations of the class 
template DeviceArray \< Data, T \> with the same argument for parameter T
and the appropriate type for parameter Data.  Valid specializations of 
RField \<D, T\> with D=1, 2, or 3 are thus derived from specializations 
of DeviceArray \< Data, T \> in which Data is the appropriate type for 
real numbers used with the chosen backend, i.e., Data=double for 
T=Pscf::CPT or Data=cudaReal for T=Pscf::CUT).  Valid specializations of 
RFieldDft \< D, T \> and CField \< D, T \> are instead derived from 
specializations of DeviceArray \< Data, T \> in which Data is the 
corresponding complex type, i.e., Data=fftw_complex for T=Pscf::CPT or 
Data=cudaComplex for T=Pscf::CUT). 

The main difference between the field and DeviceArray class templates
is that each field class stores information about the dimensions of 
the mesh on which the field is is defined, in addition to the actual
field data. These mesh dimensions is stored in each field class 
(i.e., in RField, RFieldDft and CField) as a member variable named
meshDimensions_ that is an instance of IntVec \<D\>, and that is 
returned by a public member function naned "meshDimensions".

The field and FFT class templates are are implemented by defining 
separate partial specializations for different values of the backend 
identifier class T.  The directory src/prdc/field/cuda contains a set 
of partial specializations of these classes for a template argument 
T=Pscf::CUT, which are written in CUDA C++ and enable use of a GPU. 
The directory src/prdc/cpu contains an analogous set of partial 
specializations for a template argument T=Pscf::CPT, which are written 
in standard C++ and designed to use standard CPU hardware.  

For example, the partial specialization of RField \<D,T\> for T=Pscf::CPT 
is defined in the file prdc/field/cpu/RField.h as a template
\code
namespace Pscf {
namespace Prdc {

    template <int D>
    class RField<D, CPT> : public DeviceArray<double,CPT>
    {
    public:

        // Default constructor
        RField();

        // Destructor.
        ~RField() override;

        // Allocator that also sets mesh dimensions.
        allocate(IntVec<D> const & meshDimensions);

        // Deallocate the array nullify mesh dimensions.
        deallocate();

        // Return the mesh dimensions.
        IntVec<D> const & meshDimensions() const;

        // ...

    private:

        IntVec<D> meshDimensions_;

        // ...

    }

}
}
\endcode
This defines a container that owns a block of CPU main memory. The 
corresponding partial specialization of RField \<D,T\> for T=Pscf::CUT 
is defined in file prdc/field/cuda/RField.h, and is derived from 
DeviceArray<cudaReal,Pscf::CUT>, thus creating a container that owns a 
raw array in GPU global memory. The allocate member function for either 
partial specialization of RField takes the IntVec<D> meshDimensions
as an argument and allocates an array in which the number of elements 
is equal to the product of the D elements of this vector, corresponding
to the number of nodes in the resulting mesh.

Specializations of the field class templates with T=Pscf::CPT are 
indirectly derived from the Util::Array class template, because 
DeviceArray \<Data, T \> is derived from Util::Array \< Data \>. These 
specializations thus inherit the overloaded subscript (square bracket) 
operator that is defined by the Util::Array template, which provides 
access to individual array elements on the CPU. Specializations of the 
field classes with T=Pscf::CUT instead allow access to individual 
elements only from the GPU, within CUDA kernels. 

\section developer_backend_field_fft_sec FFT Class Templates

The partial specializations of the template FFT \<D,T\> for arguments
T=Pscf::CPT and T=Pscf::CUT are wrappers around different underlying FFT 
libraries.  An instance of FFT \<D, CPT\> with D=1,2, or 3 is a wrapper 
for the open-source FFTW library, which uses standard CPU hardware. A 
corresponding instance of Pscf::Cuda::FFT \<D, CUT\> is instead a wrapper 
around the NVIDIA cufft library, which performs FFT operations on a GPU. 
These specializations provide analogous interfaces for D-dimensional 
real-to-complex, complex-to-real, and complex-to-complex transforms,
allowing them to be used interchangeably in class and function 
templates.

